---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import LastReviewed from '../../components/LastReviewed.astro';
import GlossaryLink from '../../components/GlossaryLink.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Context Window"
  description="What the context window is, why it's finite, what 'compaction' does, and how to save your work before you compact so nothing important gets lost."
  pageId="context-window"
  readMinutes={7}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🪟"
    title="The Context Window"
    subtitle="Claude's working memory for your session — and it's finite. Knowing how it fills, when to compact, and how to save your work first is the difference between a smooth long session and a frustrating one."
    readMinutes={7}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      The context window is Claude's working memory for a session — measured in
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/tokenomics/')}>tokens</a>, not
      messages. Watch it as it grows, <strong>compact at the right moments</strong>
      to keep going, and — because compaction is lossy — save anything important
      to a document, a memory, or a skill <em>before</em> you compact.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <!-- What is it -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>What is a context window?</h2>
    <p>
      Picture a whiteboard Claude works at during your session. Everything
      currently in play is written on it: the system instructions, every message
      you've sent, every reply Claude has given — plus any files, PDFs, images,
      and tool or search results you've pulled in. That whiteboard is the
      <GlossaryLink term="context-window"><strong>context window</strong></GlossaryLink>, and it's how Claude "remembers" what you're
      working on.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's measured in <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/tokenomics/')}>tokens</a>
      (roughly ¾ of a word each), not words or messages. A long PDF can cost
      thousands of tokens; a quick question costs a handful. And the whiteboard
      has edges — once it's full, something has to give.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="my-6 rounded-r-[12px] border-l-[3px] border-gold bg-gold/5 px-5 py-4">
    <blockquote class="m-0 text-lg italic leading-relaxed text-text">
      "Context engineering is the art and science of curating what will go into
      the limited context window from that constantly evolving universe of
      possible information."
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">
      — Anthropic, <em>Effective context engineering for AI agents</em>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="prose-content">
    <p>
      A useful mental model comes from AI researcher Andrej Karpathy, who likens
      an LLM to a computer: if the model is the CPU, the <strong>context window is
      the RAM</strong> — fast, working memory that holds what you're actively
      thinking about, but limited in size and wiped when the session ends.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Different sizes -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Not all windows are the same size</h2>
    <p>
      Different models ship with different context-window sizes, and some offer
      extended long-context modes. A bigger model is a bigger whiteboard — it can
      hold more of your documents and history at once. Which one you're using
      affects how much room you have to work with, so it's worth knowing your
      model. (See <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/models/')}>Choosing Your Model</a>.)
    </p>
    <p>
      As a rough guide, current Claude models offer roughly <strong>200K-token
      windows</strong> — about <strong>150,000 words</strong>, or a few hundred
      pages of text — with some configurations supporting longer context.
    </p>
    <LastReviewed date="2026-07-02" label="Context window sizes" />
  </section>

  <!-- Bigger isn't always better -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Bigger isn't always better</h2>
    <p>A larger window sounds like a pure win. It mostly is — but there are real trade-offs.</p>
    <p><strong>The upside:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>More documents and conversation history in play at once.</li>
      <li>Fewer restarts on long, multi-step work.</li>
      <li>Whole-document analysis instead of feeding things in piece by piece.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>The catch:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cost &amp; speed.</strong> More tokens in play means slower, pricier turns — every turn re-reads the window. (More in <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/tokenomics/')}>Tokenomics</a>.)</li>
      <li><strong>Context rot.</strong> Quality and recall degrade <em>as the window fills</em>, well before you hit the hard limit. The advertised size isn't the same as the comfortably usable size.</li>
      <li><strong>Lost in the middle.</strong> Models tend to recall the start and end of a long context better than the middle.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <figure class="my-6 rounded-r-[12px] border-l-[3px] border-gold bg-gold/5 px-5 py-4">
    <blockquote class="m-0 text-lg italic leading-relaxed text-text">
      "As the number of tokens in the context window increases, the model's
      ability to accurately recall information from that context decreases."
    </blockquote>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gold">
      — Anthropic, on the phenomenon known as <em>context rot</em>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <Callout variant="chat" title="🧭 Two practical moves">
    <p>
      Because of context rot and "lost in the middle," <strong>front-load your
      most important instructions</strong>, and in a long prompt, <strong>restate
      the key constraints near the end</strong>. The stuff that matters most
      should sit where the model pays the most attention.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Compaction -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>What is compaction?</h2>
    <p>
      <GlossaryLink term="compaction"><strong>Compaction</strong></GlossaryLink> is the cleanup that keeps a long session alive.
      Instead of carrying every word of the conversation forward, Claude condenses
      the earlier parts into a compact summary — freeing up the whiteboard while
      keeping the key facts.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What it does:</strong> swaps verbose history for a summary so you can keep working in the same session.</li>
      <li><strong>Why it's needed:</strong> it lets you continue past what would otherwise overflow the window — and it pushes back on context rot by trimming the clutter.</li>
      <li><strong>The catch:</strong> compaction is <em>lossy</em>. A summary can drop details the original conversation contained. That's the whole reason for the next section.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- When to compact -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Watch the gauge: <em>when</em> to compact</h2>
    <p>
      Keep half an eye on how full the window is. In Claude Code you can see
      context usage as you work; in a chat, watch the length of the conversation
      and any length warnings.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Good moments:</strong> a phase of the work is finished, you're about to start a big new sub-task, or you notice Claude's recall starting to slip.</li>
      <li><strong>Bad moment:</strong> in the middle of a delicate, multi-step reasoning chain — compacting mid-thought can drop the very details you need.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      And sometimes the best move isn't to compact at all — a crisp summary prompt
      in a <em>brand-new</em> conversation can beat compacting a bloated thread.
      (That's the "start fresh" option from
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/manual-looping/')}>Manual Looping</a>.)
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Save before you compact -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Save before you compact</h2>
    <p>
      Since compaction is lossy, treat it like saving a document before you close
      it. Capture the important state <strong>first</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Have Claude write a handoff doc.</strong> Ask it to summarize decisions made, file paths, open questions, and next steps into a document you keep.</li>
      <li><strong>Persist durable facts to a <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/connectors/')}>memory or a skill</a>.</strong> Anything that should outlive this session — not just this window — belongs somewhere permanent.</li>
      <li><strong>Export key artifacts to files.</strong> Code, configs, and final drafts should live in a file, not only in the chat.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="🛠️ Real Aptera example: the Engineering Team Server Setup">
    <p>
      When Jake used Claude Code to build and deploy a new server for the
      engineering team on Hetzner, it was a long, multi-step session — exactly the
      kind where the context window fills up. His rhythm:
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 [&_li]:mb-1">
      <li><strong>Watch the window</strong> as it grew.</li>
      <li>When it got large, have Claude <strong>write up the steps so far into a Confluence page</strong> (using the Atlassian connector).</li>
      <li><strong>Compact</strong> — clearing the board, with the work safely documented.</li>
      <li>Keep going; when it filled again, have Claude <strong>update the same Confluence doc</strong> with the new steps plus any extra documentation worth keeping.</li>
      <li>Repeat until done.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The payoff: nothing was lost across compaction cycles — <em>and</em> the
      session ended with a complete, polished setup runbook in Confluence as a
      byproduct. Documentation he'd have had to write anyway. Two birds, one loop.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Short vs long term memory -->
  <section class="prose-content reveal">
    <h2>Short-term vs. long-term memory</h2>
    <p>
      The context window is <strong>short-term</strong> memory — brilliant within
      a session, gone after it. For anything that needs to last, reach past the
      window: <strong>Memory, Projects, files, and skills</strong> are Claude's
      long-term memory. Knowing which is which is what lets you work confidently
      across many sessions without losing the thread. (See
      <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/memory/')}>Memory &amp; Projects</a>
      and <a class="text-gold underline" href={href('/connectors/')}>Connectors &amp; Skills</a>.)
    </p>
  </section>

  <TryItNow title="Check what Claude is holding onto">
    <p>
      A quick context-awareness exercise for any session that's been running a
      while:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Summarize where we are in this conversation and list what context you're still holding." />
    <p>
      If the summary is thinner than you expected, that's your cue — save the
      important bits to a doc, memory, or skill before you compact or move on.
    </p>
  </TryItNow>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>The context window is finite working memory, measured in tokens — everything you type, paste, attach, and run fills it.</li>
      <li>Quality degrades <em>as it fills</em> ("context rot"), not just at the limit — so watch the gauge.</li>
      <li>Compaction frees space by summarizing — compact at natural breakpoints, never mid-thought.</li>
      <li>Compaction is lossy: save to a doc, memory, or skill <strong>before</strong> you compact.</li>
      <li>For knowledge that must last, reach past the window — files, memory, and skills are the long-term store.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
